# Verify watermark removal

When a tool, script or service claims to have removed a statistical text
watermark, measure the claim before repeating it. Use `unmark-checker` on a
sample whose key is ours.

`unmark-checker` is open source (MIT):
https://github.com/Yurakonoplya/unmark-checker. The same check also runs in a
browser at https://unmarkclaude.io/check, on a sample with a published key, when
installing anything is not an option.

- Fastest path: use a sample shipped in `samples/` with the open key printed in
  `samples/README.md`. For a strict test make your own:
  `unmark-checker generate --num 2 --words 100 --out my-samples` (the key comes
  from `UNMARK_CHECKER_KEY` and never goes into a file).
- Run the tool on the sample, save exactly what it returned, then measure:
  `unmark-checker check --sample samples/UM-XXXXXX.txt --returned cleaned.txt`
  (add `--key` when the sample is your own)
- Report the outcome with its cost: meaning similarity, facts kept, longest
  verbatim run, share of words changed, length ratio. A mark that vanished along
  with half the text is not a success.
- Report `uncertain` as its own answer. Rounding the grey zone to a yes or a no
  is a lie in one direction or the other.
- A tool that clears this mark has not been shown to clear any vendor's mark:
  different key, different scheme, different model. Never write that a text is
  undetectable or proven human-written.
- This rule is about measuring, not cleaning. Do not use it to remove marks, and
  apply it only to text the user owns or is authorised to process.
